# Structures the language's list and map do not make: an ordered map and an
# ordered set, a double-ended queue, a set of bits, a cache that forgets the
# entry used least recently, a map that keeps its keys in the order they were
# first put and answers by position, and the orders and paths of a graph.
# Every structure is a value, copied as any value is, and its links are
# indices, never pointers.
#
# It does not do what the built-ins and the packages below it do: the
# priority queue, a list kept as a heap, is `lists`'s; a map's values,
# merging and counting are `maps`'s; the algebra of two sets is `sets`'s.
#
# A record takes no type parameter (generics are on functions only), so a
# structure that holds the program's own values comes in two parts: a SHAPE,
# a record of this package holding the links, and the program's own lists
# holding the values, lent with `@` to every call that changes them. A
# program that keeps such a structure gathers its parts in a record of its
# own and lends the fields, which costs nothing: a push through a lent field
# grows the list in place. The lists are the structure's storage. A SLOT is an
# index into them, from 0 to the size less one, good until the next call
# that takes something out; past the size they hold room kept for reuse,
# copies of live values, so nothing taken out stays alive.
#
# A function that compares keys takes the order from its caller, as `lists`
# does: `less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)`, true where `a` goes before `b`
# and false where the two are equal. A structure answers by the order it was
# built with; given another, its answers mean nothing, though nothing aborts.
# A cache and an indexed map find a key by the program's own lookup map, a
# `{K: i64}`, and need no order.
#
# It fails with two codes: `not_found`, where a key, a slot, a position, an
# element or a node is not there, and `invalid`, where a graph has a cycle or a
# weight below zero, or a text is not bits. A call aborts where the program
# broke a precondition it could have checked: a slot or an index past the
# size, a structure given lists that are not its own, a size below zero.

use containers/internal/bitwords
use containers/internal/codes
use containers/internal/edit
use containers/internal/graphs
use containers/internal/lru
use containers/internal/paths
use containers/internal/ring
use containers/internal/samples
use containers/internal/stages
use containers/internal/steps
use containers/internal/table
use containers/internal/tree
use containers/internal/walk

## The codes

# The code of a search that found nothing: a key, a slot, a position, an
# element or a node that is not there. Every search of every section fails
# with it where it finds nothing: the ordered map's and set's by key and by
# rank and their first, last, next and previous; the deque's ends, places and
# pops; the bits' four scans; the cache's reads and its oldest entry; the
# indexed map's reads by key and position and its two removals by key; a
# graph's cycle, reach and paths, and a sorter's `sorter_done` given no node.
constant NOT_FOUND: str
    codes.NOT_FOUND

# The code of an input the function does not read: a graph with a cycle, a
# weight below zero, infinite or not a number, a text that is not bits, a node
# handed back that was not handed out, an item put into a full bounded deque.
# `topological_order`, `new_sorter`, `sorter_done`, `shortest_path`,
# `distances_from`, `parse_bits` and `deque_insert` fail with it.
constant INVALID: str
    codes.INVALID

## The ordered map and the ordered set

# The shape of an ordered map or an ordered set: a binary search tree
# balanced by the sizes of its branches, whose nodes are the slots of the
# program's lists, a key at each slot of a key list and, for a map, its value
# at the same slot of a value list.
#
# Made empty by `new_ordered_map` or `new_ordered_set`, and changed only by
# this section's functions, which keep it balanced: a function that finds by
# a key calls the order about the logarithm of the size times, the deepest
# slot standing less than two and a half times that logarithm, base two, from
# the root. A map's shape refuses a set's writes, and a set's a map's, by
# aborting, since a value list would drift from its keys. `==` compares two
# shapes slot by slot, not their keys.
record Ordered
    # The tree, held by the package itself: a program reads it through this
    # section's functions.
    tree: tree.Tree

# An empty ordered map, whose program keeps an empty key list and an empty
# value list beside it.
#
# See also `new_ordered_set`, `ordered_put`.
function new_ordered_map() -> Ordered
    return Ordered(tree: tree.empty(holds_values: true))

# An empty ordered set, whose program keeps an empty key list beside it.
#
# See also `new_ordered_map`, `ordered_add`.
function new_ordered_set() -> Ordered
    return Ordered(tree: tree.empty(holds_values: false))

# How many keys an ordered map or set holds.
#
# - `ordered`: the map's or the set's shape.
#
# See also `ordered_slots`.
function ordered_size(ordered: Ordered) -> i64
    return ordered.tree.count

# Puts `value` under `key` in an ordered map, replacing the value of a key it
# holds.
#
# - `ordered`: the map's shape, made by `new_ordered_map`; it gains a slot
#   where `key` is new.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys; a new key goes at the slot after the last.
# - `value_list`: the map's values; the key's slot takes `value`.
# - `key`: the key; one equal by `less` to a key held replaces that key's
#   value, the key held staying.
# - `value`: the value to put.
# - `less`: the map's order; it is called about the logarithm of the size
#   times.
#
# Aborts where `ordered` is a set's shape or a list is shorter than its size.
# See also `ordered_get`, `ordered_remove`.
function ordered_put<K, V>(@ordered: Ordered, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], key: K, value: V, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool))
    edit.require_kind(ordered.tree, as_map: true, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    spot = edit.add(@ordered.tree, @key_list, key: key, less: less)
    edit.store(@value_list, spot.slot, value)

# The value under `key` in an ordered map.
#
# - `ordered`: the map's shape.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
# - `key`: the key to find.
# - `less`: the map's order.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the map holds no key equal to `key`. Aborts where
# `ordered` is a set's shape. See also `ordered_slot_of`, `ordered_put`.
function ordered_get<K, V>(ordered: Ordered, key_list: [K] lent, value_list: [V] lent, key: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> V?
    edit.require_kind(ordered.tree, as_map: true, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    at = walk.exact_of(ordered.tree, key_list, key: key, less: less)

    if at == tree.NONE
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"the map's {ordered.tree.count} keys hold none equal to the key asked")
    return ok(value_list[at])

# Takes `key` and its value out of an ordered map, answering the value.
#
# - `ordered`: the map's shape; it loses a slot.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys; the last slot's key moves into the slot freed.
# - `value_list`: the map's values, moved as the keys are.
# - `key`: the key to take out.
# - `less`: the map's order.
#
# Since the last slot moves, a slot held before the call may name another key
# after it. Fails `not_found` where the map holds no key equal to `key`, and
# leaves it as it was. Aborts where `ordered` is a set's shape. See also
# `ordered_remove_at`, `ordered_put`.
function ordered_remove<K, V>(@ordered: Ordered, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], key: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> V?
    edit.require_kind(ordered.tree, as_map: true, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    at = walk.exact_of(ordered.tree, key_list, key: key, less: less)

    if at == tree.NONE
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"the map's {ordered.tree.count} keys hold none equal to the key to take out")
    value = value_list[at]
    edit.take_entry(@ordered.tree, @key_list, @value_list, at)
    return ok(value)

# Takes the entry at slot `at` out of an ordered map.
#
# - `ordered`: the map's shape; it loses a slot.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys; the last slot's key moves into `at`.
# - `value_list`: the map's values, moved as the keys are.
# - `at`: the slot to free, from 0 to the size less one, as a search answered
#   it.
#
# Aborts where `at` names no slot or `ordered` is a set's shape. It is how a
# walk takes out the first or the last entry: `ordered_first`, then this.
# See also `ordered_remove`.
function ordered_remove_at<K, V>(@ordered: Ordered, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], at: i64)
    edit.require_kind(ordered.tree, as_map: true, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    tree.require_live(ordered.tree, at)
    edit.take_entry(@ordered.tree, @key_list, @value_list, at)

# Adds `key` to an ordered set, answering whether it was new.
#
# - `ordered`: the set's shape, made by `new_ordered_set`; it gains a slot
#   where `key` is new.
# - `key_list`: the set's keys; a new key goes at the slot after the last.
# - `key`: the key; one equal by `less` to a key held leaves the set as it
#   was.
# - `less`: the set's order.
#
# False where the set held an equal key already. Aborts where `ordered` is a
# map's shape. See also `ordered_discard`, `ordered_contains`.
function ordered_add<K>(@ordered: Ordered, @key_list: [K], key: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> bool
    edit.require_kind(ordered.tree, as_map: false, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: 0)
    return edit.add(@ordered.tree, @key_list, key: key, less: less).added

# Takes `key` out of an ordered set, answering whether it was there.
#
# - `ordered`: the set's shape; it loses a slot where the key was there.
# - `key_list`: the set's keys; the last slot's key moves into the slot freed.
# - `key`: the key to take out.
# - `less`: the set's order.
#
# False where the set held no equal key, and then it is left as it was.
# Aborts where `ordered` is a map's shape. See also `ordered_discard_at`,
# `ordered_add`.
function ordered_discard<K>(@ordered: Ordered, @key_list: [K], key: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> bool
    edit.require_kind(ordered.tree, as_map: false, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: 0)
    at = walk.exact_of(ordered.tree, key_list, key: key, less: less)

    if at == tree.NONE
        return false
    edit.take_key(@ordered.tree, @key_list, at)
    return true

# Takes the key at slot `at` out of an ordered set.
#
# - `ordered`: the set's shape; it loses a slot.
# - `key_list`: the set's keys; the last slot's key moves into `at`.
# - `at`: the slot to free, from 0 to the size less one.
#
# Aborts where `at` names no slot or `ordered` is a map's shape. See also
# `ordered_discard`, `ordered_first`.
function ordered_discard_at<K>(@ordered: Ordered, @key_list: [K], at: i64)
    edit.require_kind(ordered.tree, as_map: false, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: 0)
    tree.require_live(ordered.tree, at)
    edit.take_key(@ordered.tree, @key_list, at)

# Whether an ordered map or set holds a key equal to `key`.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: its keys.
# - `key`: the key to look for.
# - `less`: its order.
#
# See also `ordered_slot_of`.
function ordered_contains<K>(ordered: Ordered, key_list: [K] lent, key: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> bool
    codes.require_storage(held: key_list.len(), count: ordered.tree.count, what: "key list")
    return walk.exact_of(ordered.tree, key_list, key: key, less: less) != tree.NONE

# The slot of the key equal to `key` in an ordered map or set.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: its keys.
# - `key`: the key to find.
# - `less`: its order.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no key is equal to `key`. See also
# `ordered_contains`, `ordered_floor`.
function ordered_slot_of<K>(ordered: Ordered, key_list: [K] lent, key: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> i64?
    codes.require_storage(held: key_list.len(), count: ordered.tree.count, what: "key list")
    return codes.answer(
        at: walk.exact_of(ordered.tree, key_list, key: key, less: less),
        msg: f"no key equal to the key asked among the {ordered.tree.count} held"
    )

# The slot of the largest key at or before `key`.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: its keys.
# - `key`: the bound, held or not.
# - `less`: its order.
#
# Fails `not_found` where every key goes after `key`, an empty map or set
# included. See also `ordered_lower`, `ordered_ceiling`.
function ordered_floor<K>(ordered: Ordered, key_list: [K] lent, key: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> i64?
    codes.require_storage(held: key_list.len(), count: ordered.tree.count, what: "key list")
    return codes.answer(
        at: walk.floor_of(ordered.tree, key_list, key: key, less: less),
        msg: f"no key at or before the key asked among the {ordered.tree.count} held"
    )

# The slot of the smallest key at or after `key`.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: its keys.
# - `key`: the bound, held or not.
# - `less`: its order.
#
# Fails `not_found` where every key goes before `key`, an empty map or set
# included. See also `ordered_higher`, `ordered_floor`.
function ordered_ceiling<K>(ordered: Ordered, key_list: [K] lent, key: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> i64?
    codes.require_storage(held: key_list.len(), count: ordered.tree.count, what: "key list")
    return codes.answer(
        at: walk.ceiling_of(ordered.tree, key_list, key: key, less: less),
        msg: f"no key at or after the key asked among the {ordered.tree.count} held"
    )

# The slot of the largest key strictly before `key`.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: its keys.
# - `key`: the bound, held or not.
# - `less`: its order.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no key goes before `key`. See also `ordered_floor`.
function ordered_lower<K>(ordered: Ordered, key_list: [K] lent, key: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> i64?
    codes.require_storage(held: key_list.len(), count: ordered.tree.count, what: "key list")
    return codes.answer(
        at: walk.lower_of(ordered.tree, key_list, key: key, less: less),
        msg: f"no key before the key asked among the {ordered.tree.count} held"
    )

# The slot of the smallest key strictly after `key`.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: its keys.
# - `key`: the bound, held or not.
# - `less`: its order.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no key goes after `key`. See also `ordered_ceiling`.
function ordered_higher<K>(ordered: Ordered, key_list: [K] lent, key: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> i64?
    codes.require_storage(held: key_list.len(), count: ordered.tree.count, what: "key list")
    return codes.answer(
        at: walk.higher_of(ordered.tree, key_list, key: key, less: less),
        msg: f"no key after the key asked among the {ordered.tree.count} held"
    )

# How many keys of an ordered map or set go before `key`: its rank, held or
# not.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: its keys.
# - `key`: the key to rank.
# - `less`: its order.
#
# From 0 to the size; a held key's rank is its place from 0 in the order.
# See also `ordered_at_rank`.
function ordered_rank_of<K>(ordered: Ordered, key_list: [K] lent, key: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> i64
    codes.require_storage(held: key_list.len(), count: ordered.tree.count, what: "key list")
    return walk.rank_of(ordered.tree, key_list, key: key, less: less)

# The slot of the key with `rank` keys before it.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `rank`: the place in the order, from 0.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `rank` is below zero or at or past the size. It
# costs the logarithm of the size. See also `ordered_rank_of`.
function ordered_at_rank(ordered: Ordered, rank: i64) -> i64?
    if rank < 0 || rank >= ordered.tree.count
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"rank {rank} is outside the {ordered.tree.count} keys held")
    return ok(steps.at_rank(ordered.tree, rank))

# The slot of the smallest key.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
#
# Fails `not_found` where it is empty. See also `ordered_last`,
# `ordered_next`.
function ordered_first(ordered: Ordered) -> i64?
    return codes.answer(at: steps.first(ordered.tree), msg: f"an empty map or set has no first key")

# The slot of the largest key.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
#
# Fails `not_found` where it is empty. See also `ordered_first`,
# `ordered_previous`.
function ordered_last(ordered: Ordered) -> i64?
    return codes.answer(at: steps.last(ordered.tree), msg: f"an empty map or set has no last key")

# The slot of the key just after the one at `at`.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `at`: a slot, from 0 to the size less one.
#
# Fails `not_found` after the largest key. Aborts where `at` names no slot.
# A walk from `ordered_first` to the end costs the size in all. See also
# `ordered_previous`.
function ordered_next(ordered: Ordered, at: i64) -> i64?
    tree.require_live(ordered.tree, at)
    return codes.answer(
        at: steps.successor(ordered.tree, at),
        msg: f"no key after the last among the {ordered.tree.count} held"
    )

# The slot of the key just before the one at `at`.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `at`: a slot, from 0 to the size less one.
#
# Fails `not_found` before the smallest key. Aborts where `at` names no slot.
# See also `ordered_next`.
function ordered_previous(ordered: Ordered, at: i64) -> i64?
    tree.require_live(ordered.tree, at)
    return codes.answer(
        at: steps.predecessor(ordered.tree, at),
        msg: f"no key before the first among the {ordered.tree.count} held"
    )

# Every slot in the order of the keys.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
#
# Empty for an empty map or set. See also `ordered_slots_between`,
# `ordered_to_list`.
function ordered_slots(ordered: Ordered) -> [i64]
    return steps.in_order(ordered.tree)

# The slots of the keys from `from` up to `to`, `to` left out, in order.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: its keys.
# - `from`: the first bound, kept where held.
# - `to`: the second bound, never kept; at or before `from` it gives none.
# - `less`: its order.
#
# It costs the logarithm of the size and one step a slot answered. A bound of
# another kind, kept or left out at either end, is a walk by `ordered_next`
# from `ordered_ceiling` or `ordered_higher`. See also `ordered_slots`.
function ordered_slots_between<K>(ordered: Ordered, key_list: [K] lent, from: K, to: K, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool)) -> [i64]
    codes.require_storage(held: key_list.len(), count: ordered.tree.count, what: "key list")
    return walk.between(ordered.tree, key_list, from: from, to: to, less: less)

# The keys of an ordered map or set in their order.
#
# - `ordered`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: its keys.
#
# Empty for an empty map or set. See also `ordered_slots`.
function ordered_to_list<K>(ordered: Ordered, key_list: [K] lent) -> [K]
    codes.require_storage(held: key_list.len(), count: ordered.tree.count, what: "key list")
    held: [K] @= []

    for at in steps.in_order(ordered.tree)
        held @ held.push(key_list[at])

    return held

## The double-ended queue

# The shape of a double-ended queue: a ring over the slots of the program's
# item list, so an item goes in or comes out at either end without the
# others moving.
#
# Made by `new_deque`, `new_bounded_deque` or `deque_over`, and changed only by
# this section's functions. The item list doubles when it is full and is cut
# back when three quarters of it stand empty, so each end costs a constant
# over many calls. A bounded deque holds at most its limit: an item pushed at
# one end of a full one drops the item at the other. `==` compares two shapes,
# not their items.
record Deque
    # The ring, held by the package itself.
    ring: ring.Ring

# An empty deque without a bound, whose program keeps an empty item list
# beside it.
#
# See also `new_bounded_deque`, `deque_over`.
function new_deque() -> Deque
    return Deque(ring: ring.empty(0))

# An empty deque that holds at most `limit` items, the last ones pushed.
#
# - `limit`: the most items it holds, from 1.
#
# Aborts where `limit` is below 1. See also `new_deque`, `deque_limit`.
function new_bounded_deque(limit: i64) -> Deque
    if limit < 1
        codes.abort_with(f"a bounded deque holds at least one item, and {limit} was asked")
    return Deque(ring: ring.empty(limit))

# The shape of a deque holding `items`, the first one at the front: the list
# the program has becomes the deque's item list.
#
# - `items`: the items, front to back; empty gives an empty deque.
#
# A deque without a bound. See also `new_deque`, `deque_to_list`.
function deque_over<T>(items: [T] lent) -> Deque
    return Deque(ring: ring.Ring(head: 0, count: items.len(), limit: 0))

# How many items a deque holds.
#
# - `deque`: the shape.
#
# See also `deque_limit`.
function deque_size(deque: Deque) -> i64
    return deque.ring.count

# The most items a bounded deque holds.
#
# - `deque`: the shape.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the deque has no bound. See also
# `new_bounded_deque`.
function deque_limit(deque: Deque) -> i64?
    if deque.ring.limit == 0
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: "the deque has no bound")
    return ok(deque.ring.limit)

# Adds `value` at the back of a deque.
#
# - `deque`: the shape; it grows by one, or, bounded and full, drops its
#   front item.
# - `items`: the item list.
# - `value`: the item to add.
#
# See also `deque_push_front`, `deque_pop_back`.
function deque_push_back<T>(@deque: Deque, @items: [T], value: T)
    ring.require_items(deque.ring, items.len())
    ring.push_back(@deque.ring, @items, value)

# Adds `value` at the front of a deque.
#
# - `deque`: the shape; it grows by one, or, bounded and full, drops its back
#   item.
# - `items`: the item list.
# - `value`: the item to add.
#
# See also `deque_push_back`, `deque_pop_front`.
function deque_push_front<T>(@deque: Deque, @items: [T], value: T)
    ring.require_items(deque.ring, items.len())
    ring.push_front(@deque.ring, @items, value)

# Takes the back item out of a deque and answers it.
#
# - `deque`: the shape; it shrinks by one.
# - `items`: the item list.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the deque is empty. See also `deque_pop_front`.
function deque_pop_back<T>(@deque: Deque, @items: [T]) -> T?
    ring.require_items(deque.ring, items.len())

    if deque.ring.count == 0
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: "an empty deque has no back item")
    return ok(ring.pop_back(@deque.ring, @items))

# Takes the front item out of a deque and answers it.
#
# - `deque`: the shape; it shrinks by one.
# - `items`: the item list.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the deque is empty. See also `deque_pop_back`.
function deque_pop_front<T>(@deque: Deque, @items: [T]) -> T?
    ring.require_items(deque.ring, items.len())

    if deque.ring.count == 0
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: "an empty deque has no front item")
    return ok(ring.pop_front(@deque.ring, @items))

# The front item of a deque, left in.
#
# - `deque`: the shape.
# - `items`: the item list.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the deque is empty. See also `deque_last`.
function deque_first<T>(deque: Deque, items: [T] lent) -> T?
    return deque_get(deque, items, 0)

# The back item of a deque, left in.
#
# - `deque`: the shape.
# - `items`: the item list.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the deque is empty. See also `deque_first`.
function deque_last<T>(deque: Deque, items: [T] lent) -> T?
    return deque_get(deque, items, deque.ring.count - 1)

# The item `index` places from the front of a deque.
#
# - `deque`: the shape.
# - `items`: the item list.
# - `index`: the place, 0 at the front.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `index` is below zero or at or past the size.
# See also `deque_put`.
function deque_get<T>(deque: Deque, items: [T] lent, index: i64) -> T?
    ring.require_items(deque.ring, items.len())

    if index < 0 || index >= deque.ring.count
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"place {index} is outside a deque of {deque.ring.count} items")
    return ok(items[ring.slot_of(deque.ring, capacity: items.len(), index: index)])

# Writes `value` over the item `index` places from the front of a deque.
#
# - `deque`: the shape, left as it was.
# - `items`: the item list; one slot changes.
# - `index`: the place, 0 at the front.
# - `value`: the new item.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `index` is below zero or at or past the size, and
# writes nothing. See also `deque_get`.
function deque_put<T>(deque: Deque, @items: [T], index: i64, value: T) -> ()?
    ring.require_items(deque.ring, items.len())

    if index < 0 || index >= deque.ring.count
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"place {index} is outside a deque of {deque.ring.count} items")
    at = ring.slot_of(deque.ring, capacity: items.len(), index: index)
    items[at] @ value
    return ok()

# Puts `value` `index` places from the front of a deque, the items from there
# on moving one place back.
#
# - `deque`: the shape; it grows by one.
# - `items`: the item list.
# - `index`: the place, from 0, the front, to the size, the back.
# - `value`: the item to put.
#
# It moves the items on the nearer side of `index`, at most half of them.
# Fails `not_found` where `index` is below zero or past the size, and
# `invalid` where a bounded deque is full, as Python's `deque.insert` refuses;
# either way it changes nothing. See also `deque_remove_at`.
function deque_insert<T>(@deque: Deque, @items: [T], index: i64, value: T) -> ()?
    ring.require_items(deque.ring, items.len())

    if index < 0 || index > deque.ring.count
        return fail(
            code: NOT_FOUND,
            msg: f"place {index} is outside 0 to {deque.ring.count}, where a deque of {deque.ring.count} items takes one"
        )

    if deque.ring.limit > 0 && deque.ring.count == deque.ring.limit
        return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"the deque holds its limit of {deque.ring.limit} items")
    ring.insert(@deque.ring, @items, index, value)
    return ok()

# Takes out the item `index` places from the front of a deque and answers it,
# the items after it moving one place forward.
#
# - `deque`: the shape; it shrinks by one.
# - `items`: the item list.
# - `index`: the place, 0 at the front.
#
# It moves the items on the nearer side of `index`, at most half of them.
# Fails `not_found` where `index` is below zero or at or past the size. See
# also `deque_insert`.
function deque_remove_at<T>(@deque: Deque, @items: [T], index: i64) -> T?
    ring.require_items(deque.ring, items.len())

    if index < 0 || index >= deque.ring.count
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"place {index} is outside a deque of {deque.ring.count} items")
    return ok(ring.remove_at(@deque.ring, @items, index))

# Moves the first `by` items of a deque to its back, in their order.
#
# - `deque`: the shape.
# - `items`: the item list.
# - `by`: how many places; it counts modulo the size, and below zero turns
#   the deque the other way.
#
# It moves at most half the items, and none in a full item list. An empty
# deque stays so. See also `deque_rotate_right`, `lists.rotate_left`.
function deque_rotate_left<T>(@deque: Deque, @items: [T], by: i64)
    ring.require_items(deque.ring, items.len())

    if deque.ring.count > 0
        turn = by % deque.ring.count

        if turn < 0
            ring.rotate_left(@deque.ring, @items, turn + deque.ring.count)
        else
            ring.rotate_left(@deque.ring, @items, turn)

# Moves the last `by` items of a deque to its front, in their order: Python's
# `deque.rotate`.
#
# - `deque`: the shape.
# - `items`: the item list.
# - `by`: how many places; it counts modulo the size, and below zero turns
#   the deque the other way.
#
# It moves at most half the items. See also `deque_rotate_left`.
function deque_rotate_right<T>(@deque: Deque, @items: [T], by: i64)
    ring.require_items(deque.ring, items.len())

    if deque.ring.count > 0
        turn = by % deque.ring.count

        if turn > 0
            ring.rotate_left(@deque.ring, @items, deque.ring.count - turn)
        else
            ring.rotate_left(@deque.ring, @items, -turn)

# The items of a deque, front to back.
#
# - `deque`: the shape.
# - `items`: the item list.
#
# Empty for an empty deque. A deque turned into a list, worked on by `lists`
# and given back by `deque_over`, is how it is reversed, searched or sorted.
# See also `deque_over`.
function deque_to_list<T>(deque: Deque, items: [T] lent) -> [T]
    ring.require_items(deque.ring, items.len())
    return ring.to_list(deque.ring, items)

# Empties a deque, keeping its bound.
#
# - `deque`: the shape; it holds nothing after.
# - `items`: the item list; it is emptied too, so nothing it held stays
#   alive.
#
# See also `new_deque`.
function deque_clear<T>(@deque: Deque, @items: [T])
    deque.ring @ ring.empty(deque.ring.limit)
    items @ []

## The set of bits

# A set of bits of a fixed size, numbered from 0, held 64 to a word.
#
# Made by `new_bits`, `bits_from_indices`, `bits_from_bytes`,
# `bits_from_words` or `parse_bits`, and kept by every function with its bits
# past the size clear, so `==` is true of two sets of one size holding the
# same bits.
record Bits
    # The words, held by the package itself.
    words: bitwords.Words

# A set of `size` bits, every one clear.
#
# - `size`: how many bits, from 0.
#
# Aborts where `size` is below zero. See also `bits_with_size`,
# `parse_bits`.
function new_bits(size: i64) -> Bits
    return Bits(words: bitwords.clear_set(size))

# How many bits a set holds, set or clear.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
#
# See also `bits_count_ones`.
function bits_size(bits: Bits) -> i64
    return bits.words.size

# Whether bit `at` is set.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `at`: the bit, from 0 to the size less one.
#
# Aborts where `at` is outside the set. See also `bits_set`.
function bits_is_set(bits: Bits, at: i64) -> bool
    bitwords.require_bit(bits.words, at)
    return bitwords.is_set(bits.words, at)

# Sets bit `at`.
#
# - `bits`: the set; one bit changes, or none where it was set.
# - `at`: the bit, from 0 to the size less one.
#
# Aborts where `at` is outside the set. See also `bits_clear`, `bits_put`.
function bits_set(@bits: Bits, at: i64)
    bitwords.require_bit(bits.words, at)
    bitwords.change(@bits.words, at: at / 64, mask: 1 << at % 64, how: 0)

# Clears bit `at`.
#
# - `bits`: the set; one bit changes, or none where it was clear.
# - `at`: the bit, from 0 to the size less one.
#
# Aborts where `at` is outside the set. See also `bits_set`.
function bits_clear(@bits: Bits, at: i64)
    bitwords.require_bit(bits.words, at)
    bitwords.change(@bits.words, at: at / 64, mask: 1 << at % 64, how: 1)

# Turns bit `at` over.
#
# - `bits`: the set; one bit changes.
# - `at`: the bit, from 0 to the size less one.
#
# Aborts where `at` is outside the set. See also `bits_flip_range`.
function bits_flip(@bits: Bits, at: i64)
    bitwords.require_bit(bits.words, at)
    bitwords.change(@bits.words, at: at / 64, mask: 1 << at % 64, how: 2)

# Sets bit `at` where `value` is true and clears it where false.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `at`: the bit, from 0 to the size less one.
# - `value`: what the bit becomes.
#
# Aborts where `at` is outside the set. See also `bits_set`, `bits_clear`.
function bits_put(@bits: Bits, at: i64, value: bool)
    if value
        bits_set(@bits, at)
    else
        bits_clear(@bits, at)

# Sets the bits from `from` up to `to`, `to` left out.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `from`: the first bit, from 0.
# - `to`: the bit after the last, at most the size; equal to `from` it sets
#   none.
#
# A word at a time. Aborts where `from` is below zero, `to` past the size or
# `from` after `to`. See also `bits_clear_range`.
function bits_set_range(@bits: Bits, from: i64, to: i64)
    bitwords.require_range(bits.words, from: from, to: to)
    bitwords.change_range(@bits.words, from: from, to: to, how: 0)

# Clears the bits from `from` up to `to`, `to` left out.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `from`: the first bit, from 0.
# - `to`: the bit after the last, at most the size.
#
# Aborts where `from` is below zero, `to` past the size or `from` after `to`.
# See also `bits_set_range`.
function bits_clear_range(@bits: Bits, from: i64, to: i64)
    bitwords.require_range(bits.words, from: from, to: to)
    bitwords.change_range(@bits.words, from: from, to: to, how: 1)

# Turns over the bits from `from` up to `to`, `to` left out.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `from`: the first bit, from 0.
# - `to`: the bit after the last, at most the size.
#
# Aborts where `from` is below zero, `to` past the size or `from` after `to`.
# See also `bits_flip`.
function bits_flip_range(@bits: Bits, from: i64, to: i64)
    bitwords.require_range(bits.words, from: from, to: to)
    bitwords.change_range(@bits.words, from: from, to: to, how: 2)

# How many bits of a set are set.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
#
# A word at a time. See also `bits_size`, `bits_to_indices`.
function bits_count_ones(bits: Bits) -> i64
    return bitwords.count_ones(bits.words)

# The bits set in both sets.
#
# - `bits`: the first set.
# - `other`: the second set; a shorter one reads as clear past its size.
#
# Of the larger of the two sizes. See also `bits_or`, `bits_and_not`.
function bits_and(bits: Bits, other: Bits) -> Bits
    return Bits(words: bitwords.combine(left: bits.words, right: other.words, how: 0))

# The bits set in either set.
#
# - `bits`: the first set.
# - `other`: the second set; a shorter one reads as clear past its size.
#
# Of the larger of the two sizes. See also `bits_and`, `bits_xor`.
function bits_or(bits: Bits, other: Bits) -> Bits
    return Bits(words: bitwords.combine(left: bits.words, right: other.words, how: 1))

# The bits set in one set and not the other.
#
# - `bits`: the first set.
# - `other`: the second set; a shorter one reads as clear past its size.
#
# Of the larger of the two sizes. See also `bits_or`.
function bits_xor(bits: Bits, other: Bits) -> Bits
    return Bits(words: bitwords.combine(left: bits.words, right: other.words, how: 2))

# The bits set in `bits` and clear in `other`.
#
# - `bits`: the set to keep bits of.
# - `other`: the bits to clear; a shorter one reads as clear past its size.
#
# Of the larger of the two sizes. See also `bits_and`, `bits_not`.
function bits_and_not(bits: Bits, other: Bits) -> Bits
    return Bits(words: bitwords.combine(left: bits.words, right: other.words, how: 3))

# Every bit of a set turned over, within its size.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
#
# Of the same size; the bits past it stay clear. See also `bits_and_not`.
function bits_not(bits: Bits) -> Bits
    turned @= bits.words
    bitwords.change_range(@turned, from: 0, to: turned.size, how: 2)
    return Bits(words: turned)

# Whether no bit is set in both sets.
#
# - `bits`: the first set.
# - `other`: the second set, of any size.
#
# True where either is empty. See also `bits_and`, `bits_is_subset`.
function bits_is_disjoint(bits: Bits, other: Bits) -> bool
    return bitwords.count_ones(bitwords.combine(left: bits.words, right: other.words, how: 0)) == 0

# Whether every bit set in `bits` is set in `other`.
#
# - `bits`: the set asked about.
# - `other`: the set it may lie within, of any size: a bit of `bits` past it
#   is not within.
#
# True where `bits` has no bit set. See also `bits_is_disjoint`.
function bits_is_subset(bits: Bits, other: Bits) -> bool
    return bitwords.count_ones(bitwords.combine(left: bits.words, right: other.words, how: 3)) == 0

# Whether no bit of a set is set.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
#
# True for a set of size 0. See also `bits_is_full`, `bits_count_ones`.
function bits_is_clear(bits: Bits) -> bool
    for word in bits.words.words
        if word != 0
            return false

    return true

# Whether every bit of a set is set.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
#
# True for a set of size 0. See also `bits_is_clear`.
function bits_is_full(bits: Bits) -> bool
    return bitwords.next_of(bits.words, from: 0, clear: true) == bitwords.NONE

# The first set bit at or after `from`.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `from`: where to start; below zero starts at 0.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no bit at or after `from` is set, `from` at or past
# the size included. `bits_next_set(bits, 0)` is the first set bit. See also
# `bits_previous_set`, `bits_to_indices`.
function bits_next_set(bits: Bits, from: i64) -> i64?
    return codes.answer(
        at: bitwords.next_of(bits.words, from: from, clear: false),
        msg: f"no bit at or after {from} is set in a set of {bits.words.size}"
    )

# The first clear bit at or after `from`.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `from`: where to start; below zero starts at 0.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no bit at or after `from` is clear within the size.
# See also `bits_next_set`.
function bits_next_clear(bits: Bits, from: i64) -> i64?
    return codes.answer(
        at: bitwords.next_of(bits.words, from: from, clear: true),
        msg: f"no bit at or after {from} is clear in a set of {bits.words.size}"
    )

# The last set bit at or before `from`.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `from`: where to start; at or past the size starts at the last bit.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no bit at or before `from` is set, `from` below
# zero included. See also `bits_next_set`.
function bits_previous_set(bits: Bits, from: i64) -> i64?
    return codes.answer(
        at: bitwords.previous_of(bits.words, from: from, clear: false),
        msg: f"no bit at or before {from} is set in a set of {bits.words.size}"
    )

# The last clear bit at or before `from`.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `from`: where to start; at or past the size starts at the last bit.
#
# Fails `not_found` where no bit at or before `from` is clear. See also
# `bits_previous_set`.
function bits_previous_clear(bits: Bits, from: i64) -> i64?
    return codes.answer(
        at: bitwords.previous_of(bits.words, from: from, clear: true),
        msg: f"no bit at or before {from} is clear in a set of {bits.words.size}"
    )

# The set bits of a set, from the smallest.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
#
# Empty where none is set. A word at a time, then one step a bit set. See
# also `bits_from_indices`.
function bits_to_indices(bits: Bits) -> [i64]
    return bitwords.to_indices(bits.words)

# A set of `size` bits with the bits of `indices` set.
#
# - `size`: how many bits, from 0.
# - `indices`: the bits to set, in any order, each from 0 to `size` less
#   one; one named twice is set once.
#
# Aborts where `size` is below zero or an index is outside the set. See also
# `bits_to_indices`.
function bits_from_indices(size: i64, indices: [i64]) -> Bits
    made @= new_bits(size)

    for at in indices
        bits_set(@made, at)

    return made

# The bits of a set as bytes, bit `i` in byte `i / 8` at the place of value
# `2` to the power `i % 8`: the order of Java's `BitSet.toByteArray`.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
#
# The size divided by 8, rounded up, bytes, the last one's bits past the size
# clear; empty for size 0. See also `bits_from_bytes`.
function bits_to_bytes(bits: Bits) -> [u8]
    data: [u8] @= []
    count @= bits.words.size / 8

    if bits.words.size % 8 > 0
        count @ count + 1

    for at in range(from: 0, to: count)
        byte = bits.words.words[at / 8] >> at % 8 * 8 & 0xff
        data @ data.push(to_u8(byte).must())

    return data

# The set of `data.len() * 8` bits whose bytes are `data`, in the order of
# `bits_to_bytes`.
#
# - `data`: the bytes; empty gives a set of size 0.
#
# See also `bits_to_bytes`.
function bits_from_bytes(data: [u8]) -> Bits
    made @= bitwords.clear_set(data.len() * 8)

    for at in range(from: 0, to: data.len())
        byte = to_i64(data[at]).must()
        bitwords.change(@made, at: at / 8, mask: byte << at % 8 * 8, how: 0)

    return Bits(words: made)

# The words of a set, bit `i` at bit `i % 64` of word `i / 64`, the sign bit
# being bit 63: Java's `BitSet.toLongArray` order.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
#
# The size divided by 64, rounded up, words, the bits past the size clear.
# See also `bits_from_words`.
function bits_to_words(bits: Bits) -> [i64]
    return bits.words.words

# A set of `size` bits read from `words` in the order of `bits_to_words`.
#
# - `words`: the words; fewer than the size needs read as clear past them,
#   and their bits past the size are dropped.
# - `size`: how many bits, from 0.
#
# Aborts where `size` is below zero. See also `bits_to_words`.
function bits_from_words(words: [i64], size: i64) -> Bits
    made @= bitwords.clear_set(size)

    for at in range(from: 0, to: made.words.len())
        if at < words.len()
            made.words[at] @ words[at]

    bitwords.clip(@made)
    return Bits(words: made)

# A set written as one character a bit, `0` or `1`, bit 0 first.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
#
# Empty for size 0. See also `parse_bits`.
function format_bits(bits: Bits) -> str
    written: [str] @= []

    for at in range(from: 0, to: bits.words.size)
        if bitwords.is_set(bits.words, at)
            written @ written.push("1")
        else
            written @ written.push("0")

    return written.join("")

# The set `text` writes, one character a bit, `0` or `1`, bit 0 first.
#
# - `text`: the characters; empty gives a set of size 0.
#
# Fails `invalid` at the first character that is neither, naming it and its
# byte index. See also `format_bits`.
function parse_bits(text: str) -> Bits?
    read @= bitwords.clear_set(text.len())
    at @= 0

    for c in text.chars()
        if c == "1"
            bitwords.change(@read, at: at / 64, mask: 1 << at % 64, how: 0)
        else if c != "0"
            return fail(code: INVALID, msg: f"\"{c}\" at byte {at} of the text is neither 0 nor 1")
        at @ at + 1

    return ok(Bits(words: read))

# A copy of a set made `size` bits long: bits added are clear, bits past the
# new size dropped.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `size`: the new size, from 0.
#
# Aborts where `size` is below zero. See also `new_bits`.
function bits_with_size(bits: Bits, size: i64) -> Bits
    return Bits(words: bitwords.moved(bits.words, start: 0, size: size))

# The bits from `from` up to `to`, `to` left out, as a set of their own whose
# bit 0 is bit `from`.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `from`: the first bit, from 0.
# - `to`: the bit after the last, at most the size.
#
# Of size `to - from`. Aborts where `from` is below zero, `to` past the size
# or `from` after `to`. See also `bits_with_size`.
function bits_between(bits: Bits, from: i64, to: i64) -> Bits
    bitwords.require_range(bits.words, from: from, to: to)
    return Bits(words: bitwords.moved(bits.words, start: from, size: to - from))

# A copy of a set with each bit `i` moved to `i + by`, bits moved past the
# size dropped and the first `by` bits clear.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `by`: how many places, from 0; at or past the size it clears every bit.
#
# Of the same size. Aborts where `by` is below zero. See also
# `bits_shift_down`.
function bits_shift_up(bits: Bits, by: i64) -> Bits
    if by < 0
        codes.abort_with(f"a shift moves bits from 0 places, and {by} was asked")

    if by >= bits.words.size
        return new_bits(bits.words.size)
    return Bits(words: bitwords.moved(bits.words, start: -by, size: bits.words.size))

# A copy of a set with each bit `i` moved to `i - by`, the first `by` bits
# dropped and the last `by` bits clear.
#
# - `bits`: the set.
# - `by`: how many places, from 0; at or past the size it clears every bit.
#
# Of the same size. Aborts where `by` is below zero. See also
# `bits_shift_up`.
function bits_shift_down(bits: Bits, by: i64) -> Bits
    if by < 0
        codes.abort_with(f"a shift moves bits from 0 places, and {by} was asked")

    if by >= bits.words.size
        return new_bits(bits.words.size)
    return Bits(words: bitwords.moved(bits.words, start: by, size: bits.words.size))

## The cache that forgets the least recently used

# The shape of a cache that holds at most its capacity of entries, forgetting
# the one used least recently to make room and, where it has a time to live,
# every entry written longer ago than that. A key stands at each slot of the
# program's key list, its value at the same slot of its value list, and the
# program's lookup map names each key's slot; the three are the cache's
# storage, the lookup map holding `-1` for a key taken out until it is
# rebuilt.
#
# Time is the program's to give: a call that may expire an entry takes `now`,
# a reading of the program's own clock in any unit, and the time to live is
# in the same unit; nothing here reads a clock, so a test needs none. A cache
# without a time to live never reads `now`, and a program without a clock
# gives 0. Made by `new_cache` or `new_cache_with_ttl`; every call costs a
# constant over many calls.
record Cache
    # The links of the entries, held by the package itself.
    entries: lru.Lru

# An empty cache of at most `capacity` entries whose entries never expire.
#
# - `capacity`: the most entries it holds, from 1.
#
# Aborts where `capacity` is below 1. See also `new_cache_with_ttl`.
function new_cache(capacity: i64) -> Cache
    if capacity < 1
        codes.abort_with(f"a cache holds at least one entry, and a capacity of {capacity} was asked")
    return Cache(entries: lru.empty(capacity: capacity, ttl: 0))

# An empty cache of at most `capacity` entries, each forgotten `ttl` after it
# was last written.
#
# - `capacity`: the most entries it holds, from 1.
# - `ttl`: how long an entry lives after it was written, in the unit of the
#   `now` the program gives, from 1; reading an entry does not lengthen it.
#
# An entry written at `now` expires at `now + ttl`, past the largest `i64`
# never. Aborts where `capacity` or `ttl` is below 1. See also `new_cache`,
# `cache_expire`.
function new_cache_with_ttl(capacity: i64, ttl: i64) -> Cache
    if capacity < 1
        codes.abort_with(f"a cache holds at least one entry, and a capacity of {capacity} was asked")

    if ttl < 1
        codes.abort_with(f"a time to live is at least 1, and {ttl} was asked")
    return Cache(entries: lru.empty(capacity: capacity, ttl: ttl))

# How many entries a cache holds, those expired since the last call that was
# given the time included.
#
# - `cache`: the shape.
#
# See also `cache_capacity`, `cache_expire`.
function cache_size(cache: Cache) -> i64
    return cache.entries.count

# The most entries a cache holds.
#
# - `cache`: the shape.
#
# See also `cache_size`.
function cache_capacity(cache: Cache) -> i64
    return cache.entries.capacity

# Puts `value` under `key`, the entry becoming the one used last and, with a
# time to live, written at `now`.
#
# - `cache`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the cache's keys.
# - `value_list`: the cache's values.
# - `lookup`: the cache's lookup map.
# - `key`: the key; a held one has its value replaced.
# - `value`: the value to put.
# - `now`: the program's time, read only with a time to live.
#
# It first forgets the entries expired at `now`, then, where the cache is full
# and `key` is new, the entry used least recently. Aborts where a list or the
# lookup map is not the cache's own. See also `cache_get`.
function cache_put<K, V>(@cache: Cache, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, key: K, value: V, now: i64)
    lru.require_lists(cache.entries, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    lru.put(@cache.entries, @key_list, @value_list, @lookup, key: key, value: value, now: now)

# The value under `key`, the entry becoming the one used last.
#
# - `cache`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the cache's keys.
# - `value_list`: the cache's values.
# - `lookup`: the cache's lookup map.
# - `key`: the key to find.
# - `now`: the program's time, read only with a time to live.
#
# It first forgets the entries expired at `now`. Fails `not_found` where the
# cache holds no live entry under `key`. See also `cache_peek`,
# `cache_put`.
function cache_get<K, V>(@cache: Cache, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, key: K, now: i64) -> V?
    lru.require_lists(cache.entries, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    lru.expire(@cache.entries, @key_list, @value_list, @lookup, now)
    at = lru.slot_of(cache.entries, lookup, key)

    if at == lru.NONE
        return fail(
            code: NOT_FOUND,
            msg: f"the cache's {cache.entries.count} live entries hold none under the key asked"
        )
    lru.unlink_use(@cache.entries, at)
    lru.link_newest(@cache.entries, at)
    return ok(value_list[at])

# The value under `key`, the cache left as it was: no use is counted and
# nothing is forgotten.
#
# - `cache`: the shape.
# - `value_list`: the cache's values.
# - `lookup`: the cache's lookup map.
# - `key`: the key to find.
# - `now`: the program's time, read only with a time to live.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the cache holds no entry under `key`, or holds one
# expired at `now`. See also `cache_get`, `cache_contains`.
function cache_peek<K, V>(cache: Cache, value_list: [V] lent, lookup: {K: i64} lent, key: K, now: i64) -> V?
    codes.require_storage(held: value_list.len(), count: cache.entries.count, what: "value list")
    at = lru.slot_of(cache.entries, lookup, key)

    if at == lru.NONE || lru.is_expired(cache.entries, at: at, now: now)
        return fail(
            code: NOT_FOUND,
            msg: f"the cache's {cache.entries.count} entries hold no live one under the key asked"
        )
    return ok(value_list[at])

# Whether a cache holds a live entry under `key`, counting no use.
#
# - `cache`: the shape.
# - `lookup`: the cache's lookup map.
# - `key`: the key to look for.
# - `now`: the program's time, read only with a time to live.
#
# False for an entry expired at `now`. See also `cache_peek`.
function cache_contains<K>(cache: Cache, lookup: {K: i64} lent, key: K, now: i64) -> bool
    at = lru.slot_of(cache.entries, lookup, key)
    return at != lru.NONE && !lru.is_expired(cache.entries, at: at, now: now)

# Takes the entry under `key` out of a cache and answers its value.
#
# - `cache`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the cache's keys; the last slot's key moves into the slot
#   freed.
# - `value_list`: the cache's values, moved as the keys are.
# - `lookup`: the cache's lookup map.
# - `key`: the key to take out.
#
# An entry expired and not yet forgotten is taken out and answered too.
# Fails `not_found` where the cache holds no entry under `key`. See also
# `cache_clear`.
function cache_remove<K, V>(@cache: Cache, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, key: K) -> V?
    lru.require_lists(cache.entries, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    at = lru.slot_of(cache.entries, lookup, key)

    if at == lru.NONE
        return fail(
            code: NOT_FOUND,
            msg: f"the cache's {cache.entries.count} entries hold none under the key to take out"
        )
    value = value_list[at]
    lru.take(@cache.entries, @key_list, @value_list, @lookup, at)
    return ok(value)

# Forgets every entry expired at `now`.
#
# - `cache`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the cache's keys.
# - `value_list`: the cache's values.
# - `lookup`: the cache's lookup map.
# - `now`: the program's time; a cache without a time to live forgets
#   nothing.
#
# One step an entry forgotten. See also `new_cache_with_ttl`.
function cache_expire<K, V>(@cache: Cache, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, now: i64)
    lru.require_lists(cache.entries, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    lru.expire(@cache.entries, @key_list, @value_list, @lookup, now)

# The slots of a cache's entries, the one used last first.
#
# - `cache`: the shape.
#
# Empty for an empty cache; entries expired and not yet forgotten are among
# them. See also `cache_oldest`.
function cache_slots(cache: Cache) -> [i64]
    return lru.by_use(cache.entries)

# The slot of the entry used least recently, the next one a full cache
# forgets.
#
# - `cache`: the shape.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the cache is empty. See also `cache_slots`.
function cache_oldest(cache: Cache) -> i64?
    return codes.answer(at: cache.entries.oldest, msg: "an empty cache has no entry used least recently")

# Empties a cache, keeping its capacity and time to live.
#
# - `cache`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the cache's keys, emptied.
# - `value_list`: the cache's values, emptied.
# - `lookup`: the cache's lookup map, emptied.
#
# See also `cache_remove`.
function cache_clear<K, V>(@cache: Cache, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64})
    cache.entries @ lru.empty(capacity: cache.entries.capacity, ttl: cache.entries.ttl)
    key_list @ []
    value_list @ []
    lookup @ {}

## The map that keeps its order

# The shape of a map that keeps its keys in the order they were first put,
# each at a position from 0, as Rust's indexmap and Python's `dict` do: a key
# at each position of the program's key list, its value at the same position
# of its value list, and the program's lookup map naming each key's position.
# The three are the map's storage, the lookup map holding `-1` for a key taken
# out until it is rebuilt.
#
# Made empty by `new_indexed`. Putting, finding and taking out by swapping
# cost a constant over many calls; taking out by shifting and moving cost the
# positions passed.
record Indexed
    # The counts, held by the package itself.
    table: table.Table

# An empty map that keeps its order, whose program keeps an empty key list, an
# empty value list and an empty lookup map beside it.
#
# See also `indexed_put`.
function new_indexed() -> Indexed
    return Indexed(table: table.Table(count: 0, dead: 0))

# How many entries a map that keeps its order holds.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
#
# See also `indexed_keys`.
function indexed_size(indexed: Indexed) -> i64
    return indexed.table.count

# Puts `value` under `key` and answers its position: a new key goes last, a
# held one keeps its place and has its value replaced.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map.
# - `key`: the key.
# - `value`: the value to put.
#
# Aborts where a list or the lookup map is not the map's own. See also
# `indexed_get`, `indexed_position_of`.
function indexed_put<K, V>(@indexed: Indexed, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, key: K, value: V) -> i64
    table.require_lists(indexed.table, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    return table.put(@indexed.table, @key_list, @value_list, @lookup, key: key, value: value)

# The value under `key`.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map.
# - `key`: the key to find.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the map holds no entry under `key`. See also
# `indexed_value_at`.
function indexed_get<K, V>(indexed: Indexed, value_list: [V] lent, lookup: {K: i64} lent, key: K) -> V?
    codes.require_storage(held: value_list.len(), count: indexed.table.count, what: "value list")
    at = table.position_of(indexed.table, lookup, key)

    if at == table.GONE
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"the map's {indexed.table.count} entries hold none under the key asked")
    return ok(value_list[at])

# The position of `key`, from 0, in the order the keys were first put.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map.
# - `key`: the key to find.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the map holds no entry under `key`. See also
# `indexed_key_at`.
function indexed_position_of<K>(indexed: Indexed, lookup: {K: i64} lent, key: K) -> i64?
    return codes.answer(
        at: table.position_of(indexed.table, lookup, key),
        msg: f"the map's {indexed.table.count} entries hold none under the key asked"
    )

# Whether the map holds an entry under `key`.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map.
# - `key`: the key to look for.
#
# See also `indexed_position_of`.
function indexed_contains<K>(indexed: Indexed, lookup: {K: i64} lent, key: K) -> bool
    return table.position_of(indexed.table, lookup, key) != table.GONE

# The key at position `at`.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys.
# - `at`: the position, from 0.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `at` is below zero or at or past the size. See also
# `indexed_value_at`.
function indexed_key_at<K>(indexed: Indexed, key_list: [K] lent, at: i64) -> K?
    codes.require_storage(held: key_list.len(), count: indexed.table.count, what: "key list")

    if at < 0 || at >= indexed.table.count
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"position {at} is outside a map of {indexed.table.count} entries")
    return ok(key_list[at])

# The value at position `at`.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
# - `at`: the position, from 0.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `at` is below zero or at or past the size. See also
# `indexed_key_at`.
function indexed_value_at<V>(indexed: Indexed, value_list: [V] lent, at: i64) -> V?
    codes.require_storage(held: value_list.len(), count: indexed.table.count, what: "value list")

    if at < 0 || at >= indexed.table.count
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"position {at} is outside a map of {indexed.table.count} entries")
    return ok(value_list[at])

# Takes `key` out and answers its value, the last entry moving into its
# position: the order changes, and it costs a constant.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map.
# - `key`: the key to take out.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the map holds no entry under `key`. See also
# `indexed_shift_remove`, which keeps the order.
function indexed_swap_remove<K, V>(@indexed: Indexed, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, key: K) -> V?
    table.require_lists(indexed.table, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    at = table.position_of(indexed.table, lookup, key)

    if at == table.GONE
        return fail(
            code: NOT_FOUND,
            msg: f"the map's {indexed.table.count} entries hold none under the key to take out"
        )
    value = value_list[at]
    table.swap_remove_at(@indexed.table, @key_list, @value_list, @lookup, at)
    return ok(value)

# Takes `key` out and answers its value, every entry after it moving one
# position forward: the order is kept, at the cost of the entries moved.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map.
# - `key`: the key to take out.
#
# Fails `not_found` where the map holds no entry under `key`. See also
# `indexed_swap_remove`.
function indexed_shift_remove<K, V>(@indexed: Indexed, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, key: K) -> V?
    table.require_lists(indexed.table, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    at = table.position_of(indexed.table, lookup, key)

    if at == table.GONE
        return fail(
            code: NOT_FOUND,
            msg: f"the map's {indexed.table.count} entries hold none under the key to take out"
        )
    value = value_list[at]
    table.shift_remove_at(@indexed.table, @key_list, @value_list, @lookup, at)
    return ok(value)

# Takes the entry at position `at` out, the last entry moving into it.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map.
# - `at`: the position, from 0 to the size less one; the last one takes the
#   last entry out, Python's `dict.popitem`.
#
# Aborts where `at` names no position. See also `indexed_shift_remove_at`.
function indexed_swap_remove_at<K, V>(@indexed: Indexed, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, at: i64)
    table.require_lists(indexed.table, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    table.require_position(indexed.table, at)
    table.swap_remove_at(@indexed.table, @key_list, @value_list, @lookup, at)

# Takes the entry at position `at` out, every entry after it moving one
# position forward.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map.
# - `at`: the position, from 0 to the size less one; 0 takes the first entry
#   out.
#
# Aborts where `at` names no position. See also `indexed_swap_remove_at`.
function indexed_shift_remove_at<K, V>(@indexed: Indexed, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, at: i64)
    table.require_lists(indexed.table, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    table.require_position(indexed.table, at)
    table.shift_remove_at(@indexed.table, @key_list, @value_list, @lookup, at)

# Moves the entry at position `from` to position `to`, the entries between
# moving one position to make room.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape, left as it was.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map.
# - `from`: the entry's position.
# - `to`: its position after; the size less one moves it last, Python's
#   `OrderedDict.move_to_end`, and 0 first.
#
# It costs the positions between. Aborts where either names no position.
# See also `indexed_swap`.
function indexed_move<K, V>(indexed: Indexed, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, from: i64, to: i64)
    table.require_lists(indexed.table, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    table.require_position(indexed.table, from)
    table.require_position(indexed.table, to)
    table.move(@key_list, @value_list, @lookup, from: from, to: to)

# Swaps the entries at positions `at` and `with`.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape, left as it was.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map.
# - `at`: one position.
# - `with`: the other; equal to `at` nothing moves.
#
# Aborts where either names no position. See also `indexed_move`.
function indexed_swap<K, V>(indexed: Indexed, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, at: i64, with: i64)
    table.require_lists(indexed.table, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    table.require_position(indexed.table, at)
    table.require_position(indexed.table, with)
    table.swap(@key_list, @value_list, @lookup, at: at, with: with)

# Puts the entries in the order of their keys by `less`, entries of equal keys
# keeping their order.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map, rebuilt.
# - `less`: the order.
#
# It costs the size times its logarithm, and rebuilds the lookup map. See
# also `lists.sort_by`.
function indexed_sort_by_key<K, V>(@indexed: Indexed, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64}, less: (function(a: K, b: K) -> bool))
    table.require_lists(indexed.table, keys_held: key_list.len(), values_held: value_list.len())
    table.sort_by_key(@indexed.table, @key_list, @value_list, @lookup, less: less)

# The keys in their order.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys.
#
# Empty for an empty map. See also `indexed_values`.
function indexed_keys<K>(indexed: Indexed, key_list: [K] lent) -> [K]
    codes.require_storage(held: key_list.len(), count: indexed.table.count, what: "key list")
    return key_list.slice(from: 0, to: indexed.table.count)

# The values in the order of their keys.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `value_list`: the map's values.
#
# Empty for an empty map. See also `indexed_keys`.
function indexed_values<V>(indexed: Indexed, value_list: [V] lent) -> [V]
    codes.require_storage(held: value_list.len(), count: indexed.table.count, what: "value list")
    return value_list.slice(from: 0, to: indexed.table.count)

# Empties a map that keeps its order.
#
# - `indexed`: the shape.
# - `key_list`: the map's keys, emptied.
# - `value_list`: the map's values, emptied.
# - `lookup`: the map's lookup map, emptied.
#
# See also `new_indexed`.
function indexed_clear<K, V>(@indexed: Indexed, @key_list: [K], @value_list: [V], @lookup: {K: i64})
    indexed.table @ table.Table(count: 0, dead: 0)
    key_list @ []
    value_list @ []
    lookup @ {}

## Graphs

# A graph is a `{K: [K]}`: each key a node, and its list the nodes it points
# to, which for an order are the nodes it depends on. A node met only in a
# list is a node pointing to none; a node named twice in one list points to
# it once more, which changes no answer here. A weighted graph is a
# `{K: {K: f64}}`, each node's map giving the weight of the edge to each node
# it points to. Every function walks with a list for its stack, so a deep
# graph never runs out of stack, and costs the nodes and edges it reads.

# Every node of `graph` once: each key, followed by the nodes of its list not
# met before, in the order the graph gives them.
#
# - `graph`: the graph.
#
# The numbering `new_sorter` and its functions use: node `i` of a sorter is
# element `i` of this list. Empty for an empty graph. See also `new_sorter`.
function nodes_of<K>(graph: {K: [K]}) -> [K]
    nodes: [K] @= []
    ids: {K: i64} @= {}
    graphs.number(graph, @nodes, @ids)
    return nodes

# Every node of `graph`, each after every node it points to: a node's
# dependencies before it.
#
# - `graph`: the graph.
#
# Where several orders hold, the one Python's
# `graphlib.TopologicalSorter.static_order` gives for the same dictionary:
# the nodes ready together come together, in the order they became ready.
# Fails `invalid` where the graph has a cycle, saying how many nodes it
# holds; `cycle_of` names them. See also `cycle_of`, `new_sorter`.
function topological_order<K>(graph: {K: [K]}) -> [K]?
    nodes: [K] @= []
    numbered = graphs.numbered(graph, @nodes)
    order = graphs.staged_order(numbered)

    if order.len() < numbered.count
        loop = graphs.cycle(numbered)
        return fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: f"the graph has a cycle of {loop.len() - 1} nodes, so no order puts every node after the nodes it points to; `cycle_of` names them"
        )
    return ok(graphs.named(nodes, order))

# A cycle of `graph`: nodes each pointed to by the one after it, the first
# node standing at the end again.
#
# - `graph`: the graph.
#
# The cycle Python's `graphlib` names in its `CycleError` for the same
# dictionary; a node that points to itself is a cycle of two entries. Fails
# `not_found` where the graph has none. See also `topological_order`.
function cycle_of<K>(graph: {K: [K]}) -> [K]?
    nodes: [K] @= []
    numbered = graphs.numbered(graph, @nodes)
    loop = graphs.cycle(numbered)

    if loop.len() == 0
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"the graph's {numbered.count} nodes hold no cycle")
    return ok(graphs.named(nodes, loop))

# The strongly connected components of `graph`: the largest groups of nodes
# each reaching every other one of its group.
#
# - `graph`: the graph.
#
# Every node stands in exactly one component, alone where it is on no cycle.
# A component comes after every component it points into, so a graph with no
# cycle gives its nodes one to a component in an order `topological_order`
# could give; within a component the nodes stand in the order of `nodes_of`.
# See also `topological_order`.
function strong_components<K>(graph: {K: [K]}) -> [[K]]
    nodes: [K] @= []
    numbered = graphs.numbered(graph, @nodes)
    found: [[K]] @= []

    for component in graphs.components(numbered)
        found @ found.push(graphs.named(nodes, component))

    return found

# Every node `from` reaches by the edges of `graph`, `from` first, then the
# nearer before the farther.
#
# - `graph`: the graph.
# - `from`: the node to start at.
#
# Fails `not_found` where `from` is not a node of `graph`. See also
# `shortest_path`.
function reachable_from<K>(graph: {K: [K]}, from: K) -> [K]?
    nodes: [K] @= []
    ids: {K: i64} @= {}
    graphs.number(graph, @nodes, @ids)

    match ids[from]
        .ok start => return ok(graphs.named(nodes, graphs.reached(graphs.links(graph, nodes, ids), start)))
        .err _ => return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"the node to start at is none of the graph's {nodes.len()} nodes")

# The nodes of a path from `from` to `to` of the least total weight, both ends
# included.
#
# - `graph`: the weighted graph; every weight is finite and zero or more.
# - `from`: the node to start at.
# - `to`: the node to reach; equal to `from` it gives `[from]`.
#
# Of several paths of the least weight, the one Dijkstra's algorithm finds
# first, each node settled in the order of its distance. Fails `not_found`
# where `from` or `to` is not a node or `to` cannot be reached, and `invalid`
# where a weight is below zero, infinite or not a number. It costs the edges
# times the logarithm of the nodes. See also `distances_from`.
function shortest_path<K>(graph: {K: {K: f64}}, from: K, to: K) -> [K]?
    nodes: [K] @= []
    ids: {K: i64} @= {}
    paths.number(graph, @nodes, @ids)
    weighted = paths.weigh(graph, nodes, ids)?
    start = ids[from].default(-1)
    goal = ids[to].default(-1)

    if start == -1 || goal == -1
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"an end of the path is none of the graph's {nodes.len()} nodes")
    found = paths.settle(weighted, from: start, goal: goal)

    if found.settled[goal] == 0
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: "no path leads from the first node to the second")
    return ok(graphs.named(nodes, paths.path_to(found, goal)))

# The least total weight of a path from `from` to every node it reaches.
#
# - `graph`: the weighted graph; every weight is finite and zero or more.
# - `from`: the node to start at, at distance 0.
#
# The nodes in the order of their distance. Fails `not_found` where `from` is
# not a node, and `invalid` where a weight is below zero, infinite or not a
# number. See also `shortest_path`.
function distances_from<K>(graph: {K: {K: f64}}, from: K) -> {K: f64}?
    nodes: [K] @= []
    ids: {K: i64} @= {}
    paths.number(graph, @nodes, @ids)
    weighted = paths.weigh(graph, nodes, ids)?
    start = ids[from].default(-1)

    if start == -1
        return fail(code: NOT_FOUND, msg: f"the node to start at is none of the graph's {nodes.len()} nodes")
    found = paths.settle(weighted, from: start, goal: -1)
    distances: {K: f64} @= {}

    for node in found.order
        distances[nodes[node]] @ found.best[node]

    return ok(distances)

# A topological order handed out in stages, for work done in parallel: the
# nodes ready are handed out together, and a node becomes ready once every
# node it points to is done. Python's `graphlib.TopologicalSorter` after its
# `prepare`.
#
# Made by `new_sorter`. Its nodes are numbers, node `i` being element `i` of
# `nodes_of` of the same graph.
record Sorter
    # The stages, held by the package itself.
    stages: stages.Stages

# A sorter of `graph`'s nodes, none handed out yet.
#
# - `graph`: the graph.
#
# Fails `invalid` where the graph has a cycle, as Python's `prepare` raises
# `CycleError`. See also `sorter_ready`, `nodes_of`.
function new_sorter<K>(graph: {K: [K]}) -> Sorter?
    nodes: [K] @= []
    numbered = graphs.numbered(graph, @nodes)

    if graphs.staged_order(numbered).len() < numbered.count
        loop = graphs.cycle(numbered)
        return fail(
            code: INVALID,
            msg: f"the graph has a cycle of {loop.len() - 1} nodes, so its nodes cannot all be handed out; `cycle_of` names them"
        )
    return ok(Sorter(stages: stages.prepare(numbered)))

# The nodes become ready since the last call, now handed out: Python's
# `get_ready`.
#
# - `sorter`: the sorter; the nodes answered count as handed out.
#
# Empty where none became ready, while nodes handed out are not yet done or
# once every node is done. See also `sorter_done`.
function sorter_ready(@sorter: Sorter) -> [i64]
    return stages.hand_out(@sorter.stages)

# Marks `node` done, so the nodes that wait on it alone become ready:
# Python's `done`.
#
# - `sorter`: the sorter.
# - `node`: a node's number, as `sorter_ready` answered it.
#
# Fails `invalid` where `node` was not handed out or is done already, and
# `not_found` where it is no node's number; either way nothing changes. See
# also `sorter_ready`, `sorter_is_active`.
function sorter_done(@sorter: Sorter, node: i64) -> ()?
    return stages.finish(@sorter.stages, node)

# Whether a sorter has more to hand out or nodes handed out and not done:
# Python's `is_active`.
#
# - `sorter`: the sorter.
#
# See also `sorter_ready`.
function sorter_is_active(sorter: Sorter) -> bool
    return stages.is_active(sorter.stages)

## Examples

# An empty map, ready for names and ages.
test "new_ordered_map: an empty map of names to ages"
    ages = new_ordered_map()
    assert ordered_size(ages) == 0

# An empty set, ready for numbers.
test "new_ordered_set: an empty set of numbers"
    seen = new_ordered_set()
    assert ordered_first(seen).is_err()

# A key put twice counts once.
test "ordered_size: three keys put, one of them twice"
    ages @= new_ordered_map()
    names: [str] @= []
    years: [i64] @= []

    for name in ["eve", "bob", "eve", "ann"]
        ordered_put(@ages, @names, @years, key: name, value: 30, less: samples.by_bytes)

    assert ordered_size(ages) == 3

# A value put, then replaced under the same key.
test "ordered_put: an age put and then changed"
    ages @= new_ordered_map()
    names: [str] @= []
    years: [i64] @= []
    ordered_put(@ages, @names, @years, key: "ann", value: 30, less: samples.by_bytes)
    ordered_put(@ages, @names, @years, key: "ann", value: 31, less: samples.by_bytes)
    assert ordered_get(ages, names, years, key: "ann", less: samples.by_bytes).must() == 31

# A value found by its key, and a key that is not there.
test "ordered_get: an age by name, and a name not held"
    ages @= new_ordered_map()
    names: [str] @= []
    years: [i64] @= []
    ordered_put(@ages, @names, @years, key: "bob", value: 40, less: samples.by_bytes)
    assert ordered_get(ages, names, years, key: "bob", less: samples.by_bytes).must() == 40
    assert ordered_get(ages, names, years, key: "zoe", less: samples.by_bytes).is_err()

# A key taken out, its value answered.
test "ordered_remove: a name taken out of the map"
    ages @= new_ordered_map()
    names: [str] @= []
    years: [i64] @= []
    ordered_put(@ages, @names, @years, key: "bob", value: 40, less: samples.by_bytes)
    ordered_put(@ages, @names, @years, key: "ann", value: 30, less: samples.by_bytes)
    assert ordered_remove(@ages, @names, @years, key: "bob", less: samples.by_bytes).must() == 40
    assert ordered_to_list(ages, names) == ["ann"]

# The smallest key taken out by its slot: Rust's `pop_first`.
test "ordered_remove_at: the earliest deadline taken out"
    jobs @= new_ordered_map()
    deadlines: [i64] @= []
    titles: [str] @= []
    ordered_put(@jobs, @deadlines, @titles, key: 9, value: "late", less: samples.ascending)
    ordered_put(@jobs, @deadlines, @titles, key: 3, value: "soon", less: samples.ascending)
    at = ordered_first(jobs).must()
    assert titles[at] == "soon"
    ordered_remove_at(@jobs, @deadlines, @titles, at)
    assert ordered_to_list(jobs, deadlines) == [9]

# A number added once, and again.
test "ordered_add: a number new, then held already"
    seen @= new_ordered_set()
    numbers: [i64] @= []
    assert ordered_add(@seen, @numbers, key: 7, less: samples.ascending)
    assert !ordered_add(@seen, @numbers, key: 7, less: samples.ascending)

# A number taken out, and one that was never there.
test "ordered_discard: a number held, then one absent"
    seen @= new_ordered_set()
    numbers: [i64] @= []
    _ = ordered_add(@seen, @numbers, key: 7, less: samples.ascending)
    assert ordered_discard(@seen, @numbers, key: 7, less: samples.ascending)
    assert !ordered_discard(@seen, @numbers, key: 8, less: samples.ascending)

# The largest number taken out of a set by its slot.
test "ordered_discard_at: the largest number taken out"
    seen @= new_ordered_set()
    numbers: [i64] @= []

    for n in [4, 9, 1]
        _ = ordered_add(@seen, @numbers, key: n, less: samples.ascending)

    ordered_discard_at(@seen, @numbers, ordered_last(seen).must())
    assert ordered_to_list(seen, numbers) == [1, 4]

# Whether a word is in a set of words.
test "ordered_contains: a word held and a word not"
    words @= new_ordered_set()
    held: [str] @= []
    _ = ordered_add(@words, @held, key: "kiwi", less: samples.by_bytes)
    assert ordered_contains(words, held, key: "kiwi", less: samples.by_bytes)
    assert !ordered_contains(words, held, key: "lime", less: samples.by_bytes)

# The slot of a key, to read its value beside it.
test "ordered_slot_of: the slot of a name, then its value"
    ages @= new_ordered_map()
    names: [str] @= []
    years: [i64] @= []
    ordered_put(@ages, @names, @years, key: "ann", value: 30, less: samples.by_bytes)
    at = ordered_slot_of(ages, names, key: "ann", less: samples.by_bytes).must()
    assert years[at] == 30

# The dearest price within a budget.
test "ordered_floor: the dearest price at or under a budget"
    prices @= new_ordered_set()
    held: [i64] @= []

    for price in [30, 10, 20]
        _ = ordered_add(@prices, @held, key: price, less: samples.ascending)

    assert held[ordered_floor(prices, held, key: 25, less: samples.ascending).must()] == 20
    assert ordered_floor(prices, held, key: 5, less: samples.ascending).is_err()

# The first train at or after a time.
test "ordered_ceiling: the next train from a time"
    trains @= new_ordered_set()
    times: [i64] @= []

    for minute in [600, 615, 640]
        _ = ordered_add(@trains, @times, key: minute, less: samples.ascending)

    assert times[ordered_ceiling(trains, times, key: 610, less: samples.ascending).must()] == 615
    assert times[ordered_ceiling(trains, times, key: 615, less: samples.ascending).must()] == 615

# The last key strictly before a bound.
test "ordered_lower: the version before a given one"
    versions @= new_ordered_set()
    numbers: [i64] @= []

    for n in [1, 2, 4]
        _ = ordered_add(@versions, @numbers, key: n, less: samples.ascending)

    assert numbers[ordered_lower(versions, numbers, key: 4, less: samples.ascending).must()] == 2

# The first key strictly after a bound.
test "ordered_higher: the version after a given one"
    versions @= new_ordered_set()
    numbers: [i64] @= []

    for n in [1, 2, 4]
        _ = ordered_add(@versions, @numbers, key: n, less: samples.ascending)

    assert numbers[ordered_higher(versions, numbers, key: 2, less: samples.ascending).must()] == 4
    assert ordered_higher(versions, numbers, key: 4, less: samples.ascending).is_err()

# A score's place in a table, counted from 0.
test "ordered_rank_of: how many scores are lower"
    scores @= new_ordered_set()
    held: [i64] @= []

    for n in [50, 70, 90]
        _ = ordered_add(@scores, @held, key: n, less: samples.ascending)

    assert ordered_rank_of(scores, held, key: 70, less: samples.ascending) == 1
    assert ordered_rank_of(scores, held, key: 80, less: samples.ascending) == 2

# The median of an odd number of values.
test "ordered_at_rank: the middle of five numbers"
    values @= new_ordered_set()
    held: [i64] @= []

    for n in [9, 1, 5, 3, 7]
        _ = ordered_add(@values, @held, key: n, less: samples.ascending)

    assert held[ordered_at_rank(values, 2).must()] == 5

# The smallest key.
test "ordered_first: the earliest date"
    dates @= new_ordered_set()
    held: [i64] @= []

    for day in [20261117, 20251231]
        _ = ordered_add(@dates, @held, key: day, less: samples.ascending)

    assert held[ordered_first(dates).must()] == 20251231

# The largest key.
test "ordered_last: the latest date"
    dates @= new_ordered_set()
    held: [i64] @= []

    for day in [20261117, 20251231]
        _ = ordered_add(@dates, @held, key: day, less: samples.ascending)

    assert held[ordered_last(dates).must()] == 20261117

# A walk forwards through every key.
test "ordered_next: every word in order, one step at a time"
    words @= new_ordered_set()
    held: [str] @= []

    for word in ["pear", "fig", "date"]
        _ = ordered_add(@words, @held, key: word, less: samples.by_bytes)

    walked: [str] @= []
    at @= ordered_first(words).must()
    walked @ walked.push(held[at])

    while ordered_next(words, at).is_err() == false
        at @ ordered_next(words, at).must()
        walked @ walked.push(held[at])

    assert walked == ["date", "fig", "pear"]

# A walk backwards from the largest key.
test "ordered_previous: the key before the last"
    words @= new_ordered_set()
    held: [str] @= []

    for word in ["pear", "fig", "date"]
        _ = ordered_add(@words, @held, key: word, less: samples.by_bytes)

    at = ordered_previous(words, ordered_last(words).must()).must()
    assert held[at] == "fig"

# Every entry in the order of its key, with its value.
test "ordered_slots: names and ages in the order of the names"
    ages @= new_ordered_map()
    names: [str] @= []
    years: [i64] @= []
    ordered_put(@ages, @names, @years, key: "cy", value: 3, less: samples.by_bytes)
    ordered_put(@ages, @names, @years, key: "al", value: 1, less: samples.by_bytes)
    written: [str] @= []

    for at in ordered_slots(ages)
        written @ written.push(f"{names[at]}={years[at]}")

    assert written.join(" ") == "al=1 cy=3"

# The keys of a range, the upper bound left out.
test "ordered_slots_between: the events of a day"
    events @= new_ordered_set()
    times: [i64] @= []

    for hour in [8, 12, 17, 23, 30]
        _ = ordered_add(@events, @times, key: hour, less: samples.ascending)

    found: [i64] @= []

    for at in ordered_slots_between(events, times, from: 12, to: 24, less: samples.ascending)
        found @ found.push(times[at])

    assert found == [12, 17, 23]

# The keys in order, as a list.
test "ordered_to_list: the words of a sentence sorted and each once"
    words @= new_ordered_set()
    held: [str] @= []

    for word in ["to", "be", "or", "not", "to", "be"]
        _ = ordered_add(@words, @held, key: word, less: samples.by_bytes)

    assert ordered_to_list(words, held) == ["be", "not", "or", "to"]

# An empty queue.
test "new_deque: an empty queue of tasks"
    tasks = new_deque()
    assert deque_size(tasks) == 0

# The last three lines read kept, the older ones dropped.
test "new_bounded_deque: the last three lines of a log"
    tail @= new_bounded_deque(3)
    lines: [str] @= []

    for line in ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e"]
        deque_push_back(@tail, @lines, line)

    assert deque_to_list(tail, lines) == ["c", "d", "e"]

# A list the program has, made a deque.
test "deque_over: a list of jobs made a queue"
    jobs: [str] @= ["build", "test"]
    queue @= deque_over(jobs)
    assert deque_pop_front(@queue, @jobs).must() == "build"

# How many items a deque holds.
test "deque_size: two items pushed, one popped"
    queue @= new_deque()
    items: [i64] @= []
    deque_push_back(@queue, @items, 1)
    deque_push_back(@queue, @items, 2)
    _ = deque_pop_front(@queue, @items).must()
    assert deque_size(queue) == 1

# The bound of a bounded deque, and none of another.
test "deque_limit: a bound of ten, and no bound"
    assert deque_limit(new_bounded_deque(10)).must() == 10
    assert deque_limit(new_deque()).is_err()

# A queue served first in, first out.
test "deque_push_back: customers served in the order they came"
    queue @= new_deque()
    names: [str] @= []
    deque_push_back(@queue, @names, "ann")
    deque_push_back(@queue, @names, "bob")
    assert deque_pop_front(@queue, @names).must() == "ann"

# An urgent item put before the others.
test "deque_push_front: an urgent task put first"
    tasks @= new_deque()
    names: [str] @= []
    deque_push_back(@tasks, @names, "later")
    deque_push_front(@tasks, @names, "now")
    assert deque_first(tasks, names).must() == "now"

# A stack: the last in, the first out.
test "deque_pop_back: the last page visited, for going back"
    history @= new_deque()
    pages: [str] @= []
    deque_push_back(@history, @pages, "home")
    deque_push_back(@history, @pages, "about")
    assert deque_pop_back(@history, @pages).must() == "about"

# The front item taken out, and an empty deque's answer.
test "deque_pop_front: the next item, then none"
    queue @= new_deque()
    items: [i64] @= []
    deque_push_back(@queue, @items, 7)
    assert deque_pop_front(@queue, @items).must() == 7
    assert deque_pop_front(@queue, @items).is_err()

# The front item, left in.
test "deque_first: the next customer, not served yet"
    queue @= new_deque()
    names: [str] @= []
    deque_push_back(@queue, @names, "ann")
    assert deque_first(queue, names).must() == "ann"
    assert deque_size(queue) == 1

# The back item, left in.
test "deque_last: the newest reading"
    readings @= new_deque()
    values: [i64] @= []
    deque_push_back(@readings, @values, 18)
    deque_push_back(@readings, @values, 21)
    assert deque_last(readings, values).must() == 21

# An item by its place from the front.
test "deque_get: the second item of a queue"
    queue @= new_deque()
    items: [str] @= []

    for item in ["a", "b", "c"]
        deque_push_back(@queue, @items, item)

    assert deque_get(queue, items, 1).must() == "b"
    assert deque_get(queue, items, 3).is_err()

# An item written over in place.
test "deque_put: a queued job given a new name"
    queue @= new_deque()
    jobs: [str] @= []
    deque_push_back(@queue, @jobs, "draft")
    deque_put(queue, @jobs, 0, "final").must()
    assert deque_first(queue, jobs).must() == "final"

# An item put in the middle.
test "deque_insert: a guest seated between two others"
    row @= new_deque()
    guests: [str] @= []
    deque_push_back(@row, @guests, "ann")
    deque_push_back(@row, @guests, "cy")
    deque_insert(@row, @guests, 1, "bob").must()
    assert deque_to_list(row, guests) == ["ann", "bob", "cy"]

# An item taken out of the middle.
test "deque_remove_at: a guest who left the row"
    row @= new_deque()
    guests: [str] @= []

    for guest in ["ann", "bob", "cy"]
        deque_push_back(@row, @guests, guest)

    assert deque_remove_at(@row, @guests, 1).must() == "bob"
    assert deque_to_list(row, guests) == ["ann", "cy"]

# Turns of a round, the first player moved to the back.
test "deque_rotate_left: the next player's turn"
    round @= new_deque()
    players: [str] @= []

    for player in ["ann", "bob", "cy"]
        deque_push_back(@round, @players, player)

    deque_rotate_left(@round, @players, 1)
    assert deque_to_list(round, players) == ["bob", "cy", "ann"]

# The last items brought to the front, as Python's `rotate(1)`.
test "deque_rotate_right: the last item brought to the front"
    ring_of @= new_deque()
    numbers: [i64] @= []

    for n in [1, 2, 3, 4]
        deque_push_back(@ring_of, @numbers, n)

    deque_rotate_right(@ring_of, @numbers, 1)
    assert deque_to_list(ring_of, numbers) == [4, 1, 2, 3]

# A deque's items as a list.
test "deque_to_list: the items front to back"
    queue @= new_deque()
    items: [i64] @= []
    deque_push_back(@queue, @items, 2)
    deque_push_front(@queue, @items, 1)
    assert deque_to_list(queue, items) == [1, 2]

# A deque emptied, keeping its bound.
test "deque_clear: a bounded queue emptied"
    tail @= new_bounded_deque(2)
    lines: [str] @= []
    deque_push_back(@tail, @lines, "x")
    deque_clear(@tail, @lines)
    assert deque_size(tail) == 0
    assert deque_limit(tail).must() == 2

# A set of flags, all clear.
test "new_bits: eight clear flags"
    flags = new_bits(8)
    assert bits_count_ones(flags) == 0
    assert bits_size(flags) == 8

# The size of a set, whatever is set.
test "bits_size: a hundred bits"
    assert bits_size(new_bits(100)) == 100

# Whether one bit is set.
test "bits_is_set: a seat taken and one free"
    seats @= new_bits(10)
    bits_set(@seats, 3)
    assert bits_is_set(seats, 3)
    assert !bits_is_set(seats, 4)

# A bit set.
test "bits_set: the seventh day marked"
    days @= new_bits(31)
    bits_set(@days, 6)
    assert bits_to_indices(days) == [6]

# A bit cleared.
test "bits_clear: a seat given back"
    seats @= parse_bits("111").must()
    bits_clear(@seats, 1)
    assert format_bits(seats) == "101"

# A bit turned over.
test "bits_flip: a switch turned twice"
    switches @= new_bits(2)
    bits_flip(@switches, 0)
    assert bits_is_set(switches, 0)
    bits_flip(@switches, 0)
    assert !bits_is_set(switches, 0)

# A bit set or cleared by a boolean.
test "bits_put: a flag written from a condition"
    flags @= new_bits(4)
    bits_put(@flags, 2, true)
    bits_put(@flags, 3, false)
    assert format_bits(flags) == "0010"

# A run of bits set at once.
test "bits_set_range: the working hours of a day"
    hours @= new_bits(24)
    bits_set_range(@hours, from: 9, to: 17)
    assert bits_count_ones(hours) == 8

# A run of bits cleared at once.
test "bits_clear_range: a lunch hour taken out"
    hours @= new_bits(24)
    bits_set_range(@hours, from: 9, to: 17)
    bits_clear_range(@hours, from: 12, to: 13)
    assert bits_count_ones(hours) == 7

# A run of bits turned over.
test "bits_flip_range: the first four bits turned over"
    bits @= parse_bits("101100").must()
    bits_flip_range(@bits, from: 0, to: 4)
    assert format_bits(bits) == "010000"

# How many bits are set.
test "bits_count_ones: the seats taken"
    assert bits_count_ones(parse_bits("1101").must()) == 3

# The bits set in both.
test "bits_and: the days both are free"
    ann = parse_bits("1100").must()
    bob = parse_bits("0110").must()
    assert format_bits(bits_and(bits: ann, other: bob)) == "0100"

# The bits set in either.
test "bits_or: the days either is free"
    ann = parse_bits("1100").must()
    bob = parse_bits("0110").must()
    assert format_bits(bits_or(bits: ann, other: bob)) == "1110"

# The bits set in exactly one.
test "bits_xor: the days only one is free"
    ann = parse_bits("1100").must()
    bob = parse_bits("0110").must()
    assert format_bits(bits_xor(bits: ann, other: bob)) == "1010"

# The bits of one with those of another taken away.
test "bits_and_not: the days ann is free and bob is not"
    ann = parse_bits("1100").must()
    bob = parse_bits("0110").must()
    assert format_bits(bits_and_not(bits: ann, other: bob)) == "1000"

# Every bit turned over.
test "bits_not: the busy days from the free ones"
    assert format_bits(bits_not(parse_bits("1100").must())) == "0011"

# Whether two sets share no bit.
test "bits_is_disjoint: two shifts that do not overlap"
    morning = parse_bits("1100").must()
    evening = parse_bits("0011").must()
    assert bits_is_disjoint(bits: morning, other: evening)

# Whether one set lies within another.
test "bits_is_subset: the rights asked within the rights held"
    asked = parse_bits("0100").must()
    held = parse_bits("0110").must()
    assert bits_is_subset(bits: asked, other: held)
    assert !bits_is_subset(bits: held, other: asked)

# Whether no bit is set.
test "bits_is_clear: a new set, then one bit set"
    flags @= new_bits(3)
    assert bits_is_clear(flags)
    bits_set(@flags, 0)
    assert !bits_is_clear(flags)

# Whether every bit is set.
test "bits_is_full: a full house"
    assert bits_is_full(parse_bits("111").must())
    assert !bits_is_full(parse_bits("101").must())

# The first set bit, then the one after it.
test "bits_next_set: the first two seats taken"
    seats = parse_bits("0010010").must()
    assert bits_next_set(seats, 0).must() == 2
    assert bits_next_set(seats, 3).must() == 5

# The first clear bit: a free slot.
test "bits_next_clear: the first free slot"
    slots = parse_bits("1101").must()
    assert bits_next_clear(slots, 0).must() == 2

# The last set bit at or before a place.
test "bits_previous_set: the last seat taken before the aisle"
    seats = parse_bits("0110000").must()
    assert bits_previous_set(seats, 5).must() == 2

# The last clear bit at or before a place.
test "bits_previous_clear: the last free slot from the end"
    slots = parse_bits("0011").must()
    assert bits_previous_clear(slots, 99).must() == 1

# The places of the set bits.
test "bits_to_indices: the seats taken by number"
    assert bits_to_indices(parse_bits("1001").must()) == [0, 3]

# A set made from the places of its set bits.
test "bits_from_indices: a set of the primes below ten"
    primes = bits_from_indices(size: 10, indices: [2, 3, 5, 7])
    assert format_bits(primes) == "0011010100"

# A set written as bytes, bit 0 the lowest bit of the first byte.
test "bits_to_bytes: ten bits in two bytes"
    assert bits_to_bytes(parse_bits("1000000011").must()) == [1, 3]

# A set read from bytes.
test "bits_from_bytes: a byte of value 5"
    assert format_bits(bits_from_bytes([5])) == "10100000"

# A set written as words of 64 bits.
test "bits_to_words: bit 63 is the sign of the first word"
    flags @= new_bits(65)
    bits_set(@flags, 63)
    bits_set(@flags, 64)
    assert bits_to_words(flags) == [-9_223_372_036_854_775_808, 1]

# A set read from words.
test "bits_from_words: three bits of one word"
    assert format_bits(bits_from_words(words: [6], size: 3)) == "011"

# A set written as text.
test "format_bits: a set as zeros and ones"
    assert format_bits(bits_from_indices(size: 4, indices: [1])) == "0100"

# A set read from text, and a text that is not one.
test "parse_bits: zeros and ones, and a character that is neither"
    assert bits_count_ones(parse_bits("0110").must()) == 2

    match parse_bits("01x")
        .ok _  => assert false
        .err e => assert e.code == INVALID

# A set made longer, then shorter.
test "bits_with_size: a set grown and cut"
    flags = parse_bits("11").must()
    assert format_bits(bits_with_size(flags, 4)) == "1100"
    assert format_bits(bits_with_size(flags, 1)) == "1"

# A part of a set as a set of its own.
test "bits_between: the middle four bits"
    assert format_bits(bits_between(parse_bits("00110100").must(), from: 2, to: 6)) == "1101"

# Every bit moved up two places.
test "bits_shift_up: a pattern moved up two places"
    assert format_bits(bits_shift_up(parse_bits("1100").must(), 2)) == "0011"

# Every bit moved down one place.
test "bits_shift_down: a pattern moved down one place"
    assert format_bits(bits_shift_down(parse_bits("0110").must(), 1)) == "1100"

# A cache of two pages that never expire.
test "new_cache: a cache of two pages"
    pages = new_cache(2)
    assert cache_capacity(pages) == 2
    assert cache_size(pages) == 0

# A cache whose entries live ten units of the program's clock.
test "new_cache_with_ttl: answers kept for ten seconds"
    answers @= new_cache_with_ttl(capacity: 100, ttl: 10)
    questions: [str] @= []
    replies: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    cache_put(@answers, @questions, @replies, @lookup, key: "q", value: "a", now: 0)
    assert cache_get(@answers, @questions, @replies, @lookup, key: "q", now: 9).must() == "a"
    assert cache_get(@answers, @questions, @replies, @lookup, key: "q", now: 10).is_err()

# How many entries a cache holds.
test "cache_size: two entries put in a cache of five"
    cache @= new_cache(5)
    held: [i64] @= []
    values: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {i64: i64} @= {}
    cache_put(@cache, @held, @values, @lookup, key: 1, value: 10, now: 0)
    cache_put(@cache, @held, @values, @lookup, key: 2, value: 20, now: 0)
    assert cache_size(cache) == 2

# The most entries a cache holds.
test "cache_capacity: a cache of three"
    assert cache_capacity(new_cache_with_ttl(capacity: 3, ttl: 60)) == 3

# The entry used longest ago forgotten when the cache is full.
test "cache_put: a full cache forgets the page used longest ago"
    pages @= new_cache(2)
    urls: [str] @= []
    bodies: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    cache_put(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/a", value: "A", now: 0)
    cache_put(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/b", value: "B", now: 0)
    _ = cache_get(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/a", now: 0).must()
    cache_put(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/c", value: "C", now: 0)
    assert cache_contains(pages, lookup, key: "/a", now: 0)
    assert !cache_contains(pages, lookup, key: "/b", now: 0)

# A value read, the entry counted as used.
test "cache_get: a page read from the cache"
    pages @= new_cache(4)
    urls: [str] @= []
    bodies: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    cache_put(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/", value: "home", now: 0)
    assert cache_get(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/", now: 0).must() == "home"
    assert cache_get(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/x", now: 0).is_err()

# A value read without counting a use.
test "cache_peek: a look that leaves the order of use"
    pages @= new_cache(4)
    urls: [str] @= []
    bodies: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    cache_put(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/a", value: "A", now: 0)
    cache_put(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/b", value: "B", now: 0)
    assert cache_peek(pages, bodies, lookup, key: "/a", now: 0).must() == "A"
    assert urls[cache_oldest(pages).must()] == "/a"

# Whether a live entry is held.
test "cache_contains: a key held, then expired"
    cache @= new_cache_with_ttl(capacity: 4, ttl: 5)
    held: [i64] @= []
    values: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {i64: i64} @= {}
    cache_put(@cache, @held, @values, @lookup, key: 1, value: 1, now: 100)
    assert cache_contains(cache, lookup, key: 1, now: 104)
    assert !cache_contains(cache, lookup, key: 1, now: 105)

# An entry taken out by its key.
test "cache_remove: a stale page dropped"
    pages @= new_cache(4)
    urls: [str] @= []
    bodies: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    cache_put(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/a", value: "A", now: 0)
    assert cache_remove(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/a").must() == "A"
    assert cache_size(pages) == 0

# Every expired entry forgotten at once.
test "cache_expire: the old answers forgotten"
    cache @= new_cache_with_ttl(capacity: 4, ttl: 10)
    held: [i64] @= []
    values: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {i64: i64} @= {}
    cache_put(@cache, @held, @values, @lookup, key: 1, value: 1, now: 0)
    cache_put(@cache, @held, @values, @lookup, key: 2, value: 2, now: 5)
    cache_expire(@cache, @held, @values, @lookup, 12)
    assert cache_size(cache) == 1

# The entries from the one used last.
test "cache_slots: the pages by their last use"
    pages @= new_cache(4)
    urls: [str] @= []
    bodies: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}

    for url in ["/a", "/b", "/c"]
        cache_put(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: url, value: url, now: 0)

    order: [str] @= []

    for at in cache_slots(pages)
        order @ order.push(urls[at])

    assert order == ["/c", "/b", "/a"]

# The next entry a full cache forgets.
test "cache_oldest: the page used longest ago"
    pages @= new_cache(4)
    urls: [str] @= []
    bodies: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    cache_put(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/a", value: "A", now: 0)
    cache_put(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/b", value: "B", now: 0)
    assert urls[cache_oldest(pages).must()] == "/a"

# A cache emptied.
test "cache_clear: every page forgotten"
    pages @= new_cache(4)
    urls: [str] @= []
    bodies: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    cache_put(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup, key: "/a", value: "A", now: 0)
    cache_clear(@pages, @urls, @bodies, @lookup)
    assert cache_size(pages) == 0
    assert !cache_contains(pages, lookup, key: "/a", now: 0)

# An empty map that keeps its order.
test "new_indexed: an empty map of headers"
    headers = new_indexed()
    assert indexed_size(headers) == 0

# How many entries it holds.
test "indexed_size: two headers, one of them put twice"
    headers @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    values: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    _ = indexed_put(@headers, @names, @values, @lookup, key: "host", value: "a")
    _ = indexed_put(@headers, @names, @values, @lookup, key: "accept", value: "b")
    _ = indexed_put(@headers, @names, @values, @lookup, key: "host", value: "c")
    assert indexed_size(headers) == 2

# A new key last, a key held keeping its place.
test "indexed_put: a header added and one replaced in place"
    headers @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    values: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    assert indexed_put(@headers, @names, @values, @lookup, key: "host", value: "a") == 0
    assert indexed_put(@headers, @names, @values, @lookup, key: "accept", value: "*") == 1
    assert indexed_put(@headers, @names, @values, @lookup, key: "host", value: "b") == 0

# A value by its key.
test "indexed_get: a header's value"
    headers @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    values: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    _ = indexed_put(@headers, @names, @values, @lookup, key: "host", value: "example.org")
    assert indexed_get(headers, values, lookup, key: "host").must() == "example.org"
    assert indexed_get(headers, values, lookup, key: "age").is_err()

# The position of a key.
test "indexed_position_of: the place of a column by its name"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}

    for name in ["id", "name", "email"]
        _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: name, value: 10)

    assert indexed_position_of(columns, lookup, key: "email").must() == 2

# Whether a key is held.
test "indexed_contains: a column held and one not"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: "id", value: 4)
    assert indexed_contains(columns, lookup, key: "id")
    assert !indexed_contains(columns, lookup, key: "age")

# A key by its position.
test "indexed_key_at: the name of the second column"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}

    for name in ["id", "name"]
        _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: name, value: 8)

    assert indexed_key_at(columns, names, 1).must() == "name"
    assert indexed_key_at(columns, names, 2).is_err()

# A value by its position.
test "indexed_value_at: the width of the first column"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: "id", value: 4)
    assert indexed_value_at(columns, widths, 0).must() == 4

# A key taken out, the last entry moving into its place.
test "indexed_swap_remove: the first column taken out, the last moved in"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}

    for name in ["a", "b", "c"]
        _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: name, value: 1)

    _ = indexed_swap_remove(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: "a").must()
    assert indexed_keys(columns, names) == ["c", "b"]

# A key taken out, the order kept.
test "indexed_shift_remove: the first column taken out, the order kept"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}

    for name in ["a", "b", "c"]
        _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: name, value: 1)

    _ = indexed_shift_remove(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: "a").must()
    assert indexed_keys(columns, names) == ["b", "c"]

# The last entry taken out, Python's `popitem`.
test "indexed_swap_remove_at: the last entry popped"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}

    for name in ["a", "b"]
        _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: name, value: 1)

    last = indexed_size(columns) - 1
    assert names[last] == "b"
    indexed_swap_remove_at(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, last)
    assert indexed_keys(columns, names) == ["a"]

# The first entry taken out, the rest moving forward.
test "indexed_shift_remove_at: the oldest entry taken out"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}

    for name in ["a", "b", "c"]
        _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: name, value: 1)

    indexed_shift_remove_at(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, 0)
    assert indexed_keys(columns, names) == ["b", "c"]
    assert indexed_position_of(columns, lookup, key: "c").must() == 1

# An entry moved to the end, Python's `move_to_end`.
test "indexed_move: the first column moved last"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}

    for name in ["a", "b", "c"]
        _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: name, value: 1)

    indexed_move(columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, from: 0, to: 2)
    assert indexed_keys(columns, names) == ["b", "c", "a"]

# Two entries swapped.
test "indexed_swap: the first and last columns swapped"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}

    for name in ["a", "b", "c"]
        _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: name, value: 1)

    indexed_swap(columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, at: 0, with: 2)
    assert indexed_keys(columns, names) == ["c", "b", "a"]
    assert indexed_position_of(columns, lookup, key: "c").must() == 0

# The entries put in the order of their keys.
test "indexed_sort_by_key: columns sorted by name"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}

    for name in ["email", "id", "name"]
        _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: name, value: name.len())

    indexed_sort_by_key(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, less: samples.by_bytes)
    assert indexed_keys(columns, names) == ["email", "id", "name"]
    assert indexed_values(columns, widths) == [5, 2, 4]

# The keys in their order.
test "indexed_keys: the headers in the order they were put"
    headers @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    values: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}

    for name in ["host", "accept", "age"]
        _ = indexed_put(@headers, @names, @values, @lookup, key: name, value: "")

    assert indexed_keys(headers, names) == ["host", "accept", "age"]

# The values in the order of their keys.
test "indexed_values: the widths in the order of the columns"
    columns @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    widths: [i64] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: "b", value: 2)
    _ = indexed_put(@columns, @names, @widths, @lookup, key: "a", value: 1)
    assert indexed_values(columns, widths) == [2, 1]

# A map emptied.
test "indexed_clear: every header taken out"
    headers @= new_indexed()
    names: [str] @= []
    values: [str] @= []
    lookup: {str: i64} @= {}
    _ = indexed_put(@headers, @names, @values, @lookup, key: "host", value: "a")
    indexed_clear(@headers, @names, @values, @lookup)
    assert indexed_size(headers) == 0
    assert !indexed_contains(headers, lookup, key: "host")

# Every node of a graph, numbered.
test "nodes_of: the packages of a graph of dependencies"
    graph = {"app": ["http", "json"], "http": ["json"]}
    assert nodes_of(graph) == ["app", "http", "json"]

# Packages built after what they depend on.
test "topological_order: packages in the order they can be built"
    graph = {"app": ["http", "json"], "http": ["json"], "json": []}
    assert topological_order(graph).must() == ["json", "http", "app"]

# A cycle named, after the order refused it.
test "cycle_of: two packages that depend on each other"
    graph = {"a": ["b"], "b": ["a"]}
    assert topological_order(graph).is_err()
    assert cycle_of(graph).must() == ["a", "b", "a"]

# The groups of nodes that reach each other.
test "strong_components: two modules that use each other, and one alone"
    graph = {"a": ["b"], "b": ["a", "c"], "c": []}
    assert strong_components(graph) == [["c"], ["a", "b"]]

# Every node one reaches.
test "reachable_from: everything a package pulls in"
    graph = {"app": ["http"], "http": ["json"], "tool": ["json"]}
    assert reachable_from(graph, "app").must() == ["app", "http", "json"]

# The cheapest route through a map of towns.
test "shortest_path: the shorter road through a town between"
    roads = {"a": {"b": 1.0, "c": 5.0}, "b": {"c": 1.0}}
    assert shortest_path(roads, from: "a", to: "c").must() == ["a", "b", "c"]

# The distance to every town reached.
test "distances_from: how far each town is"
    roads = {"a": {"b": 1.0, "c": 5.0}, "b": {"c": 1.0}}
    distances = distances_from(roads, "a").must()
    assert distances["c"].must() == 2.0
    assert keys(distances) == ["a", "b", "c"]

# A build in stages: what can start now, then what follows.
test "new_sorter: a build whose first stage is the shared library"
    graph = {"app": ["lib"], "test": ["lib"], "lib": []}
    nodes = nodes_of(graph)
    sorter @= new_sorter(graph).must()
    first: [str] @= []

    for node in sorter_ready(@sorter)
        first @ first.push(nodes[node])

    assert first == ["lib"]

# The nodes ready, handed out together.
test "sorter_ready: two jobs ready together once the library is done"
    graph = {"app": ["lib"], "test": ["lib"], "lib": []}
    nodes = nodes_of(graph)
    sorter @= new_sorter(graph).must()

    for node in sorter_ready(@sorter)
        sorter_done(@sorter, node).must()

    second: [str] @= []

    for node in sorter_ready(@sorter)
        second @ second.push(nodes[node])

    assert second == ["app", "test"]

# A node marked done, and one marked done twice.
test "sorter_done: a job done once, refused the second time"
    graph: {str: [str]} = {"a": []}
    sorter @= new_sorter(graph).must()
    node = sorter_ready(@sorter)[0]
    sorter_done(@sorter, node).must()
    assert sorter_done(@sorter, node).is_err()

# Whether a sorter has more to do.
test "sorter_is_active: active until every job is done"
    graph: {str: [str]} = {"a": []}
    sorter @= new_sorter(graph).must()
    assert sorter_is_active(sorter)
    sorter_done(@sorter, sorter_ready(@sorter)[0]).must()
    assert !sorter_is_active(sorter)
